73;1;assistant;2091;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Mandibular prognathia" describes a craniofacial structural abnormality characterized by the forward projection (protrusion) of the mandible (lower jaw) relative to the maxilla (upper jaw) or the cranial base.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:** The phenotype specifically refers to a skeletal malformation where the mandible is positioned more anteriorly than normal. This includes cases where the lower jaw protrudes beyond the upper jaw, leading to an underbite (Class III malocclusion) or facial asymmetry. It encompasses both mild and severe degrees of mandibular protrusion, regardless of etiology (genetic, developmental, or acquired).   - **Excluded:** The concept does not include isolated dental malocclusions without underlying skeletal displacement (e.g., dental Class III without mandibular prognathia), nor does it include retrognathia (receding mandible) or maxillary prognathia. It is distinct from general facial asymmetry unless the asymmetry is primarily due to mandibular forward displacement.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Mandible (specifically its position and orientation in the craniofacial complex).   - **Developmental Feature:** Abnormal development or growth of the mandibular bone during craniofacial morphogenesis.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** A key feature in craniofacial dysmorphology, often associated with syndromic conditions (e.g., Pierre Robin sequence, Treacher Collins syndrome, or certain genetic disorders) but also observed in non-syndromic cases.   - **Physiological Process:** Disruption in the coordinated growth of the craniofacial skeleton, particularly involving the mandibular condyle and ramus.  **Conclusion:**   "Mandibular prognathia" refers specifically to a structural phenotype involving the anterior displacement of the mandible, with clear anatomical and developmental grounding. It is a well-defined, morphologically specific concept within the HPO, focused on skeletal jaw position rather than dental alignment or other craniofacial features.
