103;1;assistant;2483;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Retinal degeneration" refers to a progressive loss of structure and function of the retina, the light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye responsible for converting visual stimuli into neural signals. This phenotype encompasses pathological changes such as photoreceptor cell death, retinal layer thinning, and associated visual impairment.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Degenerative processes affecting the retina, including both rod and cone photoreceptors.     - Progressive deterioration of retinal architecture (e.g., loss of outer nuclear layer, disorganization of photoreceptor outer segments).     - Conditions leading to reduced or lost vision due to retinal pathology, regardless of the underlying genetic, metabolic, or acquired cause.     - Both inherited (e.g., retinitis pigmentosa, Leber congenital amaurosis) and non-inherited (e.g., age-related macular degeneration, toxic retinopathies) forms, provided they manifest as retinal degeneration.    - **Excluded:**     - Non-degenerative retinal abnormalities (e.g., retinal detachment without atrophy, retinal hemorrhage, or inflammatory changes without progressive cell loss).     - Disorders primarily affecting the optic nerve (e.g., optic atrophy) or visual pathways outside the retina.     - Transient or reversible retinal dysfunction (e.g., functional visual loss without structural degeneration).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Retina (specifically photoreceptors, retinal pigment epithelium, and retinal layers).   - **Physiological Process:** Phototransduction, retinal metabolism, and maintenance of retinal architecture.   - **Developmental Feature:** Not limited to developmental stages, can occur at any age, though often manifests in childhood (inherited forms) or later life (e.g., age-related degeneration).   - **Clinical Characteristic:** Progressive visual loss, night blindness, peripheral vision loss, reduced visual acuity, and eventual blindness in severe cases.    **Conclusion:**   "Retinal degeneration" is a broad, high-level HPO phenotype describing a progressive, structural breakdown of the retina leading to functional impairment. It is not disease-specific but captures a shared pathological outcome across diverse etiologies. Its scope includes any condition resulting in irreversible retinal tissue loss, with emphasis on the degenerative nature of the process rather than the cause.
