104;1;assistant;2078;**Phenotype Identified:**   The HPO concept "Color vision defect" refers to a reduced or absent ability to distinguish colors, particularly in the red-green or blue-yellow spectral ranges, due to dysfunction or absence of one or more types of cone photoreceptors in the retina.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Deficiencies in one or more cone types (e.g., protanopia, deuteranopia, tritanopia).     - Partial or complete loss of color discrimination, regardless of severity.     - Congenital or acquired forms (though most HPO annotations focus on inherited forms).     - Phenotypes where color perception is impaired but visual acuity and other aspects of vision may be normal.   - **Excluded:**     - General visual impairment or reduced visual acuity unrelated to color perception.     - Conditions primarily affecting rod photoreceptors (e.g., night blindness).     - Non-visual perceptual disorders or cognitive issues affecting color naming without actual sensory deficit.     - Normal color vision or mild, non-clinical variations in color perception.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Retina, specifically the cone photoreceptors (L-cones, M-cones, S-cones).   - **Physiological Process:** Phototransduction in cone cells and color signal processing in the retina and visual cortex.   - **Developmental Feature:** Typically congenital, often X-linked recessive (e.g., due to mutations in *OPN1LW* or *OPN1MW* genes), though acquired forms may occur due to disease, injury, or drugs.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** Impaired color discrimination, detectable via standardized tests (e.g., Ishihara plates, Farnsworth-Munsell 100 hue test).    **Conclusion:**   "Color vision defect" is a well-defined, specific phenotype in the HPO that refers to a disorder of color perception due to abnormal or missing cone photoreceptors, primarily affecting the retinal sensory system. It is distinct from other visual impairments and is characterized by a failure in color discrimination without necessarily affecting other visual functions.
