133;1;assistant;2462;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept labeled "Spasticity" refers to a motor disorder characterized by an increase in muscle tone that results in stiffness and resistance to passive movement. This is a velocity-dependent phenomenon, meaning the resistance increases with the speed of the movement.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:** The concept encompasses the clinical hallmark of spasticity—hypertonia with velocity-dependent resistance to passive stretch, often associated with upper motor neuron lesions. It includes both focal (e.g., in a limb) and generalized forms, as well as spasticity arising from various underlying neurological conditions (e.g., cerebral palsy, spinal cord injury, multiple sclerosis).   - **Excluded:** It does not include other forms of hypertonia such as rigidity (which is velocity-independent, as seen in Parkinson’s disease) or dystonia (which involves sustained or intermittent muscle contractions causing abnormal postures). It also excludes transient or non-neurological causes of muscle stiffness (e.g., muscle fatigue or contractures due to disuse).   - The concept is specific to the motor system and is not intended to capture subjective sensations of tightness or stiffness unrelated to objective increases in muscle tone.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Primarily affects skeletal muscles and their associated motor pathways, particularly involving the corticospinal tract and spinal motor neurons.   - **Physiological Process:** Reflects dysfunction in the regulation of muscle tone due to disruption of descending inhibitory pathways from the brain.   - **Developmental Feature:** Can be present from infancy (e.g., in congenital cerebral palsy) or develop at any age following acquired neurological injury.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** A key feature in upper motor neuron syndromes, often accompanied by hyperreflexia, clonus, and pathological reflexes (e.g., Babinski sign), though t