search and publications:** Used to define phenotypic inclusion criteria (e.g., "Infants with birthweight >90th percentile for gestational age were included").   - **Patient education materials:** **Rarely used** in plain language. Patients and families are unlikely to encounter this exact phrasing in educational handouts, because it is too technical and statistical. Instead, they are more likely to see: *"The baby was larger than most babies of the same gestational age."*   - **Conversations between patients and providers:** **Unlikely** to be used verbatim. Providers may explain the concept using simpler language (e.g., "your baby was on the high end of normal weight for how far along you were"), but would not typically say "birthweight >90th percentile" in spoken interaction.  **4. Assessment of Usage Dominance:**   - Although the phrase is **clinically precise and widely used in technical contexts**, it is **not a layperson synonym**.   - The **majority of real-world use** occurs in **clinical documentation, genetics reports, and biomedical research**—domains dominated by healthcare professionals and researchers.   - While patients may eventually understand the concept behind it, they do not use or hear this exact wording in everyday communication.  **Conclusion:**   The synonym *"Birthweight > 90th percentile"* is **not a layperson term** but a **technical, quantitatively precise descriptor** used almost exclusively by **healthcare professionals and researchers** in clinical and scientific contexts. It is most commonly found in **structured clinical documentation, genetics reports, and biomedical publications**, where accuracy and standardization are essential. Its use reflects a focus on objective measurement rather than patient communication. Therefore, **healthcare professionals account for the vast majority of its real-world usage**, and it functions as a **standardized, computable expression** of the HPO concept *Large for gestational age*, rather than a patient-friendly rewording.
