141;1;assistant;2408;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Overgrowth" refers to a phenotype characterized by an abnormal increase in body size, growth rate, or tissue mass that exceeds normal developmental expectations. This includes excessive linear growth (e.g., tall stature), increased body weight, or disproportionate enlargement of body parts or organs.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Generalized overgrowth (e.g., increased height, weight, head circumference beyond normal percentiles).     - Disproportionate overgrowth (e.g., macrocephaly, organomegaly, limb overgrowth).     - Overgrowth as a feature in syndromic conditions (e.g., Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, Sotos syndrome, Simpson-Golabi-Behmel syndrome).     - Abnormal increase in cell number (hyperplasia) or cell size (hypertrophy) contributing to increased tissue mass.    - **Excluded:**     - Normal variation in growth (e.g., tall stature due to genetics without pathological overgrowth).     - Obesity due to metabolic or dietary factors alone (unless part of a broader overgrowth syndrome).     - Temporary or reversible growth acceleration (e.g., due to hormonal therapy or catch-up growth).     - Isolated macrocephaly without overall body overgrowth (unless explicitly linked).    The concept is broad but not nonspecific—it captures pathological or clinically significant overgrowth, particularly when it is part of a developmental or genetic disorder. It does not specify the underlying cause (e.g., genetic, hormonal, neoplastic), but rather focuses on the observable phenotypic outcome.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical structure:** Whole body, specific organs (e.g., liver, kidneys), limbs, head.   - **Developmental feature:** Abnormal growth trajectory during prenatal or postnatal development.   - **Physiological process:** Dysregulation of growth signaling pathways (e.g., IGF1, PI3K-AKT-mTOR).   - **Clinical characteristic:** Often associated with congenital anomalies, developmental delay, and increased cancer risk in syndromic contexts.    **Conclusion:**   "Overgrowth" in HPO denotes a clinically significant, abnormal increase in body size or tissue mass, typically indicating a developmental or genetic disorder. It is a high-level phenotype used to capture a wide range of pathological growth abnormalities, with relevance in genetic syndromes, developmental biology, and clinical genetics.
