146;1;assistant;2417;**Phenotype Identified:**   The HPO concept "Atrial septal defect" describes a congenital heart defect characterized by an abnormal opening in the atrial septum—the wall that separates the left and right atria of the heart.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:** A persistent or congenital defect in the interatrial septum, allowing abnormal shunting of blood between the left and right atria (typically left-to-right at rest). This includes various subtypes such as ostium secundum, ostium primum, sinus venosus, and coronary sinus defects, as these are all anatomically and pathophysiologically related to the same core phenotype.   - **Excluded:** Defects involving the ventricular septum (e.g., ventricular septal defect), patent ductus arteriosus, or other non-septal cardiac anomalies. Also excluded are transient or acquired defects not present at birth, and anatomical variants without functional significance (e.g., small, non-hemodynamically significant foramen ovale in adults).   - The concept is specific to structural abnormalities of the atrial septum, with emphasis on the presence of a persistent opening that disrupts normal cardiac septation.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Atrial septum (specifically the interatrial septum), including its components (e.g., fossa ovalis, primum, secundum, sinus venosus regions).   - **Developmental Feature:** A failure of normal embryonic septation during heart development (typically during weeks 5–8 of gestation), resulting in incomplete closure of the interatrial septum.   - **Physiological Process:** Abnormal intracardiac shunting of blood, leading to volume overload of the right side of the heart and pulmonary circulation, potentially resulting in right ventricular hypertrophy, pulmonary hypertension, and, in severe cases, Eisenmenger syndrome.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** Often asymptomatic in early life but may present with signs such as a systolic ejection murmur, fixed 