157;1;assistant;2126;**Phenotype Identified:**   "Postaxial foot polydactyly" refers to the presence of an extra digit (toe) on the postaxial side of the foot — that is, the lateral side, corresponding to the little toe (fifth toe) — in addition to the normal five toes.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - A supernumerary toe located on the lateral (postaxial) aspect of the foot.     - The extra digit may be a small, rudimentary toe (e.g., a small nubbin or a fully formed toe with bones and joints).     - The condition may occur unilaterally or bilaterally.     - It is specifically restricted to the foot, polydactyly in the hand is not included unless explicitly stated.   - **Excluded:**     - Preaxial polydactyly (extra digit on the medial side, near the big toe).     - Central polydactyly (extra digit in the middle of the foot).     - Polydactyly in the hand.     - Non-structural or non-anatomical variants (e.g., skin tags or syndactyly mistaken for extra digits).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Foot, specifically the postaxial (lateral) region of the tarsal and metatarsal bones.   - **Developmental Feature:** A congenital malformation arising from abnormal limb bud patterning during embryogenesis, particularly involving the Shh (Sonic hedgehog) signaling pathway in the zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) of the limb bud.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** A structural anomaly present at birth, typically diagnosed clinically or radiologically. It may be isolated or part of a broader genetic syndrome (e.g., Bardet-Biedl syndrome, Ellis-van Creveld syndrome, or other ciliopathies).   - **Phenotypic Category:** Congenital malformation of the skeletal system, specifically of the foot.    **Conclusion:**   The HPO concept "Postaxial foot polydactyly" describes a specific, well-defined congenital structural anomaly involving the presence of an extra toe on the lateral side of the foot. It is distinct from other forms of polydactyly by location (postaxial), site (foot), and developmental origin. The concept is precise in scope and excludes polydactyly in other limbs or locations.
