lydactyly" is also acceptable.  - **Patient education materials:**     - **Not used.** This term is too technical for patients. Layperson synonyms would instead use phrases like *"extra toe on the outside of the foot"* or *"extra little toe on both feet."*     - The term "postaxial polydactyly of feet" would be confusing or intimidating to non-specialists.  - **Conversations between patients and providers:**     - **Rarely used.** Providers may use the term in written notes but would typically simplify it when speaking (e.g., *"extra toe on the outside of the foot"*).     - The term is not part of everyday patient-provider dialogue.  ---  ### **Why this synonym is preferred over the HPO’s preferred label:**  - **Grammatical flow:** "Postaxial polydactyly of feet" is more natural in English than "postaxial foot polydactyly," which can sound slightly awkward due to the compound noun "foot polydactyly."   - **Plural specificity:** "Feet" explicitly indicates bilateral involvement, which is common in genetic syndromes. The singular "foot" in the HPO label may imply unilateral or unspecified laterality, though context usually clarifies this.   - **Standardization in genetics literature:** The structure "X of Y" (e.g., "polydactyly of feet") is a well-established pattern in clinical genetics for specifying anatomical location.  ---  ### **Conclusion:**  The synonym **"Postaxial polydactyly of feet"** is **primarily used by healthcare professionals and geneticists** in clinical documentation, genetics reports, and biomedical publications. It is **not used in patient education or spoken communication** due to its technical nature. While not a layperson synonym, it is a **more natural and conventionally accepted phrasing** in clinical and research contexts than the HPO’s preferred label. Its usage reflects standard English syntax and precision in anatomical specification, making it the dominant term in real-world biomedical communication for this phenotype.
