158;1;assistant;2547;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Abnormal metatarsal morphology" describes a structural anomaly in the metatarsal bones of the foot. This refers to any deviation from the typical shape, size, alignment, or configuration of one or more of the five metatarsal bones (bones that connect the tarsal bones of the midfoot to the phalanges of the toes).  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:** Any morphological abnormality affecting the metatarsals, such as shortening, elongation, widening, curvature (e.g., metatarsus varus or valgus), fusion (synostosis), duplication, or malformation (e.g., congenital malformation, hypoplasia, or dysplasia). This includes both isolated and syndromic presentations.   - **Excluded:** Functional or biomechanical issues (e.g., plantar fasciitis, gait abnormalities) unless directly linked to a structural metatarsal anomaly. Also excluded are abnormalities of other foot bones (e.g., tarsals, phalanges) unless specifically involving the metatarsals.   - The concept is **moderately specific**—it captures a broad category of structural defects in the metatarsals but does not specify the exact nature of the abnormality (e.g., not "short metatarsal" or "metatarsus adductus" unless those are child terms). It is intended to be used when the precise morphological detail is unknown or when a general abnormality is observed.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical structure:** Metatarsal bones (specifically, the five long bones of the forefoot).   - **Developmental feature:** May arise during embryonic development (e.g., due to disruptions in limb bud patterning) or postnatally (e.g., due to trauma or disease).   - **Clinical relevance:** Often observed in congenital syndromes (e.g., Apert syndrome, Larsen syndrome), skeletal dysplasias, or isolated limb malformations. It may contribute to foot deformities, gait disturbances, or pain, but the phenotype itself focuses solely on the structural abnormality.   - **Physiological process:** Not directly related to function (e.g., muscle contraction, nerve conduction), but may indirectly affect biomechanics due to altered foot structure.  **Conclusion:**   "Abnormal metatarsal morphology" refers to any non-standard structural configuration of the metatarsal bones, encompassing a wide range of developmental or acquired malformations. It is a phenotypic descriptor used in the context of congenital or acquired skeletal anomalies, primarily in the forefoot, and serves as a parent term for more specific morphological abnormalities.
