166;1;assistant;2181;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Aplasia/Hypoplasia of toe" describes a congenital structural abnormality characterized by the complete absence (aplasia) or underdevelopment (hypoplasia) of one or more toes.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Absence (aplasia) or underdevelopment (hypoplasia) of any toe (hallux, second, third, fourth, or fifth toe), either unilaterally or bilaterally.     - The abnormality is present from birth and affects the structural formation of the toe(s), not merely functional or positional issues.     - Applies regardless of whether the defect involves the entire toe or specific components (e.g., phalanges, metatarsal, or distal segments).    - **Excluded:**     - Isolated digital anomalies not involving true aplasia/hypoplasia (e.g., syndactyly, polydactyly, or minor malformations without significant reduction in size or absence).     - Postnatal loss or acquired absence (e.g., due to trauma or infection).     - Generalized limb hypoplasia or malformation not specifically localized to the toe(s).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** The distal lower limb, specifically the foot and its individual toes.   - **Developmental Feature:** A defect in the embryonic development of the foot, particularly during the formation of the autopod (distal limb bud), where the toe elements fail to form properly or are significantly reduced in size.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** A structural congenital anomaly that may be isolated or part of a broader syndrome involving limb or skeletal malformations.   - **Relevance:** This phenotype is relevant in the context of genetic disorders, congenital limb malformation syndromes, and developmental biology, particularly when evaluating phenotypic patterns in patients with skeletal dysplasias or limb reduction defects.  **Conclusion:**   The concept refers specifically to a congenital absence or underdevelopment of one or more toes due to impaired embryonic development, with clear anatomical and developmental boundaries. It is a structural, not functional, phenotype and is distinct from other digit anomalies such as polydactyly or syndactyly.
