179;1;assistant;1966;**Phenotype Identified:**   "Poliosis" refers to the presence of a localized or generalized patch of white or gray hair due to the absence or reduction of melanin in the hair shaft.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Hair that appears white or gray in a distinct patch (localized) or across the scalp/body (generalized).     - The condition is specifically defined by the lack of pigmentation in hair, not by other hair abnormalities (e.g., alopecia, texture changes).     - Can affect any hair-bearing area (scalp, eyebrows, eyelashes, body hair), but is typically observed in visible areas.     - May be congenital or acquired, and may be associated with genetic syndromes, autoimmune conditions, or trauma.    - **Excluded:**     - Hair loss (alopecia) without pigment change.     - Generalized graying of hair due to aging (senescence), unless it is premature and patchy.     - Other pigmentary abnormalities in skin or eyes unless co-occurring.     - Non-pigmentary hair changes (e.g., brittle hair, alopecia areata).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Hair follicles and hair shafts, specifically the melanocytes responsible for pigment production.   - **Physiological Process:** Melanogenesis (melanin synthesis and deposition in hair).   - **Developmental Feature:** Can be congenital (present at birth) or develop later in life (acquired).   - **Clinical Characteristic:** A visible, non-inflammatory, depigmented hair patch, often used as a diagnostic clue in genetic or autoimmune disorders (e.g., Waardenburg syndrome, Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada syndrome, piebaldism).    **Conclusion:**   "Poliosis" is a precise phenotype describing localized or generalized depigmentation of hair due to absent or dysfunctional melanin production in hair follicles. It is distinct from general graying (senescence) and hair loss, and is clinically significant as a marker of underlying genetic, autoimmune, or developmental conditions.
