193;1;assistant;2332;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Immunodeficiency" refers to a condition characterized by a reduced or absent ability of the immune system to mount an effective response against pathogens, leading to increased susceptibility to infections.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:** The concept encompasses any impairment in immune function, whether due to defects in innate immunity, adaptive immunity (T cells, B cells, or both), or specific immune components (e.g., antibodies, complement proteins). This includes primary (genetic) immunodeficiencies, secondary (acquired) immunodeficiencies (e.g., due to HIV, chemotherapy, or malnutrition), and combined immunodeficiencies.   - **Excluded:** The term does not refer to transient immune suppression (e.g., post-surgical), normal immune variation, or non-immunological causes of infection susceptibility (e.g., anatomical barriers, poor hygiene). It also excludes isolated deficiencies in specific immune components unless they are part of a broader immunodeficiency syndrome.   - **Level of specificity:** "Immunodeficiency" is a high-level, general phenotype (a parent term in HPO) and is not specific to a particular immune cell type, pathogen susceptibility, or genetic cause. It serves as a broad category under which more specific immunodeficiencies (e.g., "B-cell immunodeficiency", "T-cell immunodeficiency") are classified.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Affected system:** Immune system   - **Key processes:** Immune response, pathogen recognition, immune cell activation, antibody production, cellular immunity   - **Clinical relevance:** Central to the diagnosis and classification of primary immunodeficiency diseases (PIDs), infectious disease susceptibility, and immune dysregulation disorders.   - **Anatomical/developmental context:** Can manifest at any age, but often presents in infancy or early childhood in genetic forms. Affects systemic immune function rather than a specific organ, though secondary organ damage may occur due to recurrent infections.  **Conclusion:**   "Immunodeficiency" in HPO denotes a broad, systemic impairment of immune function that increases infection risk. It is a general phenotype used to group more specific immune defects and is foundational for phenotypic classification in genetic and clinical contexts.
