198;1;assistant;2443;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita" refers to a congenital condition characterized by multiple joint contractures present at birth, affecting two or more areas of the body. The term "arthrogryposis" denotes the presence of fixed, abnormal flexion or extension deformities of joints, while "multiplex" indicates involvement of multiple joints, and "congenita" specifies that the condition is present from birth.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Congenital (present at birth) joint contractures affecting multiple joints (e.g., limbs, spine, face).     - Non-progressive joint stiffness due to reduced fetal movement (fetal akinesia) or intrinsic joint pathology.     - Typically involves both upper and lower limbs, but may also affect the spine, neck, or facial muscles.     - Associated with a wide range of underlying causes (genetic, environmental, or mechanical), including neuromuscular, connective tissue, or central nervous system disorders.    - **Excluded:**     - Isolated joint contractures (e.g., single joint involvement) are not included unless part of a broader multiplex pattern.     - Contractures developing postnatally due to trauma, infection, or acquired conditions.     - Joint stiffness due to muscle atrophy or spasticity without congenital contractures.    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Joints (especially in limbs, spine, and face), musculature, and associated connective tissues.   - **Developmental Feature:** Abnormal fetal movement (fetal akinesia) during gestation, which leads to joint immobility and subsequent contracture formation.   - **Physiological Process:** Impaired fetal motility, resulting in failure of normal joint development and soft tissue remodeling.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** Fixed joint deformities present at birth, often associated with short limbs, camptodactyly, clubfoot, or facial dysmorphism depending on the underlying et