206;1;assistant;2388;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Tibial bowing" refers to a structural abnormality characterized by a lateral or medial curvature of the tibia bone, resulting in a visible or measurable bow-like deformity of the lower leg. This is a skeletal malformation specifically involving the tibia.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Any abnormal curvature of the tibia (typically anterior-posterior or medial-lateral) that deviates from the normal straight alignment.     - Congenital or acquired forms, though the term is most commonly used in the context of developmental or congenital skeletal dysplasias.     - The deformity may be unilateral or bilateral and can vary in severity (mild to severe).     - Associated with conditions such as metaphyseal chondrodysplasias (e.g., metaphyseal dysplasia, Schmid type), osteogenesis imperfecta, and other genetic skeletal disorders.    - **Excluded:**     - Bowing of other long bones (e.g., femur, radius) is not included, even if present in the same patient.     - Generalized limb deformities or soft tissue abnormalities (e.g., muscle contractures) are not part of this concept unless they are secondary to tibial bowing.     - Temporary or physiological bowing in early childhood (e.g., normal infantile genu varum) is excluded, as "tibial bowing" in HPO refers to pathological or persistent structural deformity.    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Tibia (specifically the shaft or diaphysis of the tibia).   - **Developmental Feature:** Often arises during fetal development or early postnatal growth, particularly in the context of genetic skeletal disorders.   - **Physiological/Pathological Process:** Reflects abnormal endochondral ossification, impaired bone modeling, or mechanical stress due to underlying metabolic or genetic defects.   - **Clinical Relevance:** A key diagnostic feature in several inherited skeletal dysplasias, may lead to gait abnormalities, limb length discrepancies, or joint stress if severe.    **Conclusion:**   "Tibial bowing" in the HPO denotes a specific, pathological structural deformity of the tibia characterized by persistent, abnormal curvature, primarily relevant in the context of congenital or genetic skeletal disorders. It is distinct from normal developmental variations and focuses on the tibia as the primary anatomical site of pathology.
