223;1;assistant;3053;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Distal amyotrophy" refers to a neurological phenotype characterized by progressive muscle weakness and atrophy that primarily affects the distal muscles of the limbs—specifically the hands and feet—while sparing the proximal muscles (e.g., shoulders and hips). This pattern of involvement is a key diagnostic feature.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Progressive degeneration of lower motor neurons in the distal portions of the upper and lower limbs.     - Clinical manifestations such as weakness, wasting (atrophy), and reduced muscle bulk in the hands (e.g., intrinsic hand muscles) and feet (e.g., foot dorsiflexors, toe extensors).     - Preservation of proximal muscle strength and function.     - Typically spares sensory function, although some forms may have associated sensory involvement (e.g., in certain hereditary motor and sensory neuropathies).    - **Excluded:**     - Proximal muscle weakness (this distinguishes it from other forms of motor neuron disease like spinal muscular atrophy or ALS).     - Significant sensory deficits unless part of a broader syndrome (e.g., in Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease).     - Central nervous system involvement (e.g., cognitive or cerebellar signs).    The concept is specific to a **distal-predominant pattern of motor neuron degeneration**, and it does not imply a specific etiology (e.g., it may be idiopathic, hereditary, or associated with various genetic or acquired causes).  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Lower motor neurons in the anterior horn of the spinal cord and peripheral nerves innervating distal muscles.   - **Physiological Process:** Motor neuron degeneration leading to denervation, muscle atrophy, and weakness.   - **Developmental Feature:** Not inherently developmental, typically manifests in adulthood (though age of onset varies by underlying cause).   - **Clinical Characteristic:** Asymmetric or symmetric distal limb weakness and atrophy, often with normal or near-normal reflexes in the distal limbs (though reflexes may be diminished).    **Note on Ambiguity Resolution:**   The term "distal amyotrophy" is used in HPO to capture a clinical phenotype pattern rather than a specific disease. It is a parent term that includes conditions such as hereditary distal spinal muscular atrophy (e.g., HSAN1, HSAN2), certain forms of Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease (e.g., CMT2), and isolated distal amyotrophy (e.g., "distal hereditary motor neuropathy" or "Kugelberg-Welander-like" phenotypes). The HPO concept is intentionally broad to encompass this phenotypic pattern across etiologies.  **Conclusion:**   "Distal amyotrophy" describes a **phenotype of progressive, distal-predominant lower motor neuron dysfunction** affecting the hands and feet, with preserved proximal strength and typically intact sensation. It is a clinically defined pattern used to group disorders with similar neurophysiological and anatomical features, regardless of underlying genetic or pathological cause.
