ions**: Appears in case reports and cohort studies to describe the inheritance pattern (e.g., “All cases were sporadic, with no prior family history”).     - **Patient-Provider Conversations**: Used by clinicians when explaining why genetic testing may be recommended (e.g., “Since there’s no previous family history, this may be a new mutation”).  - **Wording Analysis and Contemporary English Usage**:     - *"No previous family history"* is **neutral, precise, and clinically conventional**. It avoids emotional or ambiguous language (e.g., “no one in the family” is less formal, “no family history” is common but slightly less specific).     - The phrase is **not overly technical**, but it is **not layperson-friendly** in the strict sense of a *HPO layperson synonym*. It is a **descriptive clinical phrase**—a standard term in medical documentation—used by professionals to convey a specific epidemiological fact.     - It is **not typically used by patients** in self-reporting or informal discussion, where simpler phrasing like “No one in my family has had this” is more common.  **Conclusion**:   The synonym *"No previous family history"* is **primarily used by healthcare professionals** in clinical, genetic, and research contexts to denote the absence of familial recurrence—aligning with the HPO concept *Sporadic*. While it is **plain in structure**, it is **not a layperson synonym** in the HPO sense, rather, it is a **standard clinical descriptor** used in documentation, genetics reports, and publications. The **majority of real-world usage** comes from **clinicians and geneticists**, not patients. Its use reflects professional communication norms, emphasizing precision and consistency in medical records and research.
