re standardized, precise terminology. | | **Biomedical publications** | ❌ Not used. Scientific writing demands clinical precision, this phrasing is too informal and ambiguous. |  ---  ### **Why this wording is effective as a layperson synonym:**  - **Concrete and visual:** “Pointing in a different direction” is a highly intuitive, image-based description. It relies on a shared understanding of normal finger alignment. - **Non-technical:** Avoids terms like “deviation,” “angulation,” “contracture,” or “malalignment,” which may be unfamiliar to non-specialists. - **Contextually accurate:** It captures the core perceptual feature of the phenotype—misalignment—without implying causation, severity, or anatomical detail. - **Natural language:** Mirrors how people actually describe physical anomalies in daily conversation (e.g., “My hand looks funny,” “My finger bends the wrong way”).  ---  ### **Conclusion:**  The synonym *“Finger pointing in a different direction than usual”* is **primarily used by patients and their families** to describe the HPO concept *“Deviation of finger”*. It is **most commonly encountered in patient education materials and informal clinical conversations**, where clarity and relatability are prioritized over precision. While clinicians may use it occasionally to communicate with patients, it is **not used in formal medical or scientific documentation**. Its strength lies in its natural, descriptive quality—making it an effective layperson synonym that reflects how non-experts perceive and articulate visible physical differences.
