arily relevant in radiological and genetic diagnostics, particularly when evaluating spinal anomalies in congenital or developmental disorders.  **Conclusion:**   "Biconcave vertebral bodies" denotes a precise morphological abnormality of the vertebral body endplates, characterized by concavity on both superior and inferior surfaces. It is a structural phenotype with implications in developmental biology and clinical genetics, particularly in the context of skeletal dysplasias and spinal malformations.
