272;1;assistant;2177;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Aplasia/Hypoplasia of the tibia" describes a congenital structural abnormality characterized by the complete absence (aplasia) or underdevelopment (hypoplasia) of the tibia, the larger and stronger of the two bones in the lower leg.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Complete absence of the tibia (aplasia), including cases where the bone is entirely missing or rudimentary.     - Partial underdevelopment of the tibia (hypoplasia), such as shortened, thin, or malformed tibial shafts.     - Phenotypes affecting the tibia regardless of whether the fibula is also involved (e.g., in cases of tibial hemimelia or other limb malformations).     - Occurs during embryonic development and is present at birth.    - **Excluded:**     - Postnatal bone loss or acquired tibial defects (e.g., due to trauma, infection, or tumor).     - Isolated shortening of the tibia due to growth plate dysfunction in childhood (e.g., epiphyseal dysplasia), unless developmental in origin.     - Hypoplasia of other bones (e.g., fibula, femur) unless specifically associated with tibial involvement.    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Tibia (shin bone), specifically the long bone of the lower leg.   - **Developmental Feature:** Congenital malformation arising during embryogenesis, typically in the first trimester.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** Often associated with limb reduction defects, such as tibial hemimelia, and may be part of broader syndromes (e.g., Holt-Oram syndrome, fibular hemimelia with tibial aplasia, or isolated tibial aplasia).   - **Physiological Process:** Disruption in the development of the limb bud mesenchyme and subsequent endochondral ossification of the tibial anlage.    **Conclusion:**   The phenotype refers specifically to a congenital absence or underdevelopment of the tibia, rooted in developmental biology and distinct from postnatal or acquired bone abnormalities. I