320;1;assistant;2259;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Total ophthalmoplegia" refers to the complete paralysis or loss of function of all extraocular muscles, resulting in the inability to move the eye in any direction (i.e., no voluntary movement of the eye in any gaze position).  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:** Full loss of ocular motility in all directions of gaze (up, down, left, right, and oblique movements), affecting all six extraocular muscles (superior, inferior, medial, lateral rectus, superior, and inferior oblique). This implies complete paresis or paralysis of the ocular muscles, typically due to neurological or muscular dysfunction.   - **Excluded:** Partial ophthalmoplegia (e.g., involvement of only some muscles or directions of gaze), isolated cranial nerve palsies (e.g., third nerve palsy with sparing of some movements), or non-oculomotor causes of eye movement restriction (e.g., mechanical restriction due to orbital trauma or fibrosis).   - The term is highly specific in its clinical manifestation: it denotes total, not partial, loss of eye movement.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Extraocular muscles and their innervating cranial nerves (particularly the oculomotor nerve [CN III], trochlear nerve [CN IV], and abducens nerve [CN VI]), or the neuromuscular junctions and muscles themselves.   - **Physiological Process:** Ocular motility and voluntary eye movement control.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** A complete loss of eye movement, often associated with neuromuscular disorders (e.g., mitochondrial diseases like MELAS, myasthenia gravis, congenital ophthalmoplegias, or brainstem lesions).   - **Developmental Feature:** Can be congenital (e.g., in syndromes like Kearns-Sayre or mitochondrial encephalomyopathies) or acquired (e.g., due to inflammatory, vascular, or degenerative conditions).  **Conclusion:**   "Total ophthalmoplegia" is a precise clinical phenotype describing the complete absence of voluntary eye movement due to dysfunction of the extraocular muscles or their neural control. It is distinct from partial or progressive forms and implies a severe impairment of ocular motility with broad implications for neurological, muscular, or mitochondrial pathophysiology.
