328;1;assistant;2057;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Premature ovarian insufficiency" (HP:0000814) describes a clinical condition characterized by the loss of ovarian function before the age of 40. This includes amenorrhea (absence of menstruation), elevated gonadotropin levels (FSH > 25 IU/L on two occasions at least 4 weeks apart), and reduced fertility due to diminished ovarian reserve.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Ovarian failure occurring prior to age 40.     - Clinical features such as irregular or absent menstrual cycles, infertility, and signs of estrogen deficiency (e.g., hot flashes, vaginal dryness).     - Biochemical evidence of hypergonadotropic hypogonadism (elevated FSH, low estradiol).     - Both sporadic and familial cases, including those associated with genetic conditions (e.g., Turner syndrome, Fragile X premutation, autoimmune disorders).    - **Excluded:**     - Normal menopause (which typically occurs around age 50).     - Temporary amenorrhea due to non-ovarian causes (e.g., stress, hypothalamic dysfunction, pituitary disorders).     - Ovarian failure occurring after age 40.    The concept is specific to premature loss of ovarian function and does not encompass general infertility or menstrual irregularities without evidence of ovarian failure.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Ovaries (primary organ affected).   - **Physiological Process:** Ovarian endocrine function (estrogen and progesterone production), folliculogenesis, and gonadotropin feedback regulation.   - **Developmental Feature:** Normal ovarian development and follicular reserve maintenance into early adulthood.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** A syndrome with reproductive and systemic manifestations due to estrogen deficiency, including increased risk of osteoporosis, cardiovascular disease, and cognitive changes.    This phenotype is a well-defined clinical entity within reproductive endocrinology and is used in genetic and clinical research to identify underlying causes of early ovarian aging.
