335;1;assistant;2131;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Unilateral renal atrophy" describes a condition characterized by the shrinkage (atrophy) of one kidney, with the other kidney remaining normal in size or unaffected.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Atrophy (reduction in size and functional capacity) of a single kidney.     - The atrophy is unilateral, meaning it affects only one side of the body (left or right).     - The contralateral (opposite) kidney is typically normal in size and function.     - The underlying cause (e.g., congenital, post-infectious, obstructive, ischemic) is not specified, so the concept encompasses all etiologies leading to unilateral kidney shrinkage.    - **Excluded:**     - Bilateral renal atrophy (both kidneys affected).     - Generalized renal hypoplasia or developmental underdevelopment (unless unilateral).     - Temporary or reversible shrinkage not meeting the criteria for atrophy.     - Atrophy of other organs or tissues (e.g., renal parenchymal changes not involving overall organ size).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Kidney (specifically, one kidney).   - **Pathological Process:** Atrophy (loss of tissue mass and function due to cell loss or degeneration).   - **Clinical Relevance:** Often associated with chronic kidney disease, prior renal ischemia, obstructive uropathy, congenital anomalies, or post-infectious scarring (e.g., post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis).   - **Developmental Feature:** Can be congenital (present at birth) or acquired (developing later in life).   - **Physiological Impact:** May lead to compensatory hypertrophy of the contralateral kidney, but overall renal function may remain within normal limits if the remaining kidney is healthy.  **Conclusion:**   "Unilateral renal atrophy" refers specifically to the pathological reduction in size of one kidney, with the other kidney intact. It is a structural phenotype involving the kidney, with implications for renal function and potential underlying etiologies, but does not specify cause, timing, or functional status beyond the morphological change.
