338;1;assistant;2489;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Childhood-onset short-trunk short stature" describes a specific pattern of short stature characterized by disproportionate shortening of the trunk (axial skeleton) that becomes apparent during childhood. This phenotype is distinct from generalized short stature or limb shortening (e.g., rhizomelic or mesomelic shortening).  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Short stature with onset in childhood (typically before age 10–12 years).     - Predominant shortening of the trunk (vertebral column and thorax), leading to a short trunk-to-leg ratio.     - Disproportionate short stature, where trunk length is disproportionately short relative to limb length.     - Often associated with skeletal dysplasias or congenital disorders affecting axial skeleton development.    - **Excluded:**     - Generalized short stature without trunk disproportion (e.g., in growth hormone deficiency or idiopathic short stature).     - Short stature due primarily to limb shortening (e.g., achondroplasia, which is typically rhizomelic).     - Short stature with onset in adulthood.     - Cases where trunk shortening is secondary to non-skeletal causes (e.g., severe respiratory disease or neuromuscular disorders causing poor posture, unless the short trunk is structural).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Axial skeleton (vertebral column, thorax), particularly the spine and rib cage.   - **Developmental Feature:** Abnormal development or growth of the trunk during childhood, suggesting a developmental defect in vertebral body formation or segmentation.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** Disproportionate short stature with a short trunk, often detectable on physical examination or radiographic imaging (e.g., reduced vertebral body height, short thoracic spine).   - **Associated Conditions:** Likely seen in skeletal dysplasias such as some forms of spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia, short-rib polydactyly syndromes, or other congenital disorders affecting endochondral ossification in the axial skeleton.    **Conclusion:**   The phenotype refers to a genetically or developmentally determined condition in which short stature manifests in childhood due to structural shortening of the trunk, with the spine and thorax being the primary affected anatomical regions. It is a highly specific descriptor of disproportionate short stature with axial predominance, distinguishing it from other forms of short stature.
