346;1;assistant;2376;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Small epiphysis of the proximal phalanx of the 5th finger" describes a congenital or developmental abnormality characterized by a reduced size (hypoplasia) of the epiphysis—the rounded end of the proximal phalanx of the little (fifth) finger.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Specificity:** The concept is highly specific, targeting a precise anatomical location: the proximal phalanx of the fifth digit (little finger). It further specifies the epiphysis, which is the ossification center involved in longitudinal bone growth and joint formation.   - **Included:** The phenotype includes any deviation from normal size of the epiphysis of this specific bone segment, regardless of whether it is due to genetic, developmental, or environmental factors. It refers to the morphological feature (size) of the epiphysis, not the overall length of the phalanx or other bones.   - **Excluded:** The concept does not include abnormalities of the shaft (diaphysis), metaphysis, or other phalanges (e.g., distal phalanx), nor does it encompass general shortening of the finger or syndromic features unless directly linked to this specific epiphyseal abnormality. It also excludes non-anatomical or functional manifestations (e.g., reduced mobility) unless they are secondary to the epiphyseal size.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** The epiphysis of the proximal phalanx of the fifth finger, part of the hand skeleton.   - **Developmental Feature:** This phenotype is a developmental anomaly, likely arising during endochondral ossification, where the epiphysis forms and grows. Its small size may reflect delayed or impaired ossification, growth plate dysfunction, or genetic disruption of skeletal development.   - **Clinical Relevance:** This finding may be observed in skeletal dysplasias, congenital limb malformation syndromes, or isolated skeletal anomalies. It is a morphological marker that may contribute to