b—doctors call it complete duplication of thumb bones.”  - **Genetics reports and clinical documentation:**     **Rarely used.** Clinicians and geneticists typically use precise terms like “complete duplication of the distal phalanx of the thumb” or “duplicated thumb phalanx” in formal reports. “Thumb bones” is too vague for clinical accuracy—there are multiple bones in the thumb (phalanges and metacarpal), so the term could be misinterpreted.  - **Phenotype databases and biomedical publications:**     **Not used.** These contexts require anatomical precision. “Phalanx” is the correct term, “bones” is too broad and ambiguous. Using “thumb bones” would risk confusion with duplication of the metacarpal or multiple phalanges.  - **Biomedical publications:**     Avoided due to lack of specificity. Authors would use “phalanx” or “distal phalanx” to maintain precision.  ---  ### **Assessment of usage frequency and dominance:**  - While **both patients and clinicians** may understand the phrase, **patients and families** are by far the **majority users** of this exact wording in real-world communication. - The phrase is **not used by clinicians in formal documentation** due to its imprecision. - It is **not used in databases or research** where standardization and specificity are required. - Therefore, **the majority of real-world use of this synonym occurs in patient-centered contexts**, where clarity and accessibility outweigh technical precision.  ---  ### **Conclusion:**  The synonym **“Complete duplication of thumb bones”** is **primarily used by patients and their families** to describe the HPO concept *“Complete duplication of thumb phalanx”* in **lay, patient-friendly communication**. It reflects a natural, intuitive way of expressing a visible physical anomaly using common language. While it lacks the anatomical precision of the clinical term, it is **widely understood and commonly used in patient education, support groups, and informal clinical conversations**—making it a **true layperson synonym** in the HPO sense.
