432;1;assistant;2354;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Aplasia/Hypoplasia of the breasts" refers to the congenital absence (aplasia) or underdevelopment (hypoplasia) of the breast tissue, including the mammary glands and associated structures (e.g., nipple, areola, subcutaneous fat). This phenotype is characterized by a failure of normal breast development during embryogenesis or early postnatal life.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Complete absence of breast tissue (aplasia), including the nipple-areola complex.     - Partial or reduced development of breast tissue (hypoplasia), resulting in small or underdeveloped breasts.     - Affects both breasts (bilateral) or one breast (unilateral), though the concept does not specify laterality unless further qualified.     - Applies to both males and females, though clinical relevance is typically higher in females due to reproductive and aesthetic implications.    - **Excluded:**     - Acquired breast atrophy (e.g., due to aging, weight loss, or post-lactational changes).     - Breast asymmetry not due to developmental failure.     - Isolated nipple or areola abnormalities without concomitant breast tissue deficiency.     - Breast development that is delayed but ultimately normal (e.g., constitutional delay in puberty).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Breast tissue, including mammary gland, nipple, areola, and subcutaneous adipose tissue.   - **Developmental Feature:** Failure of normal embryonic development of the mammary ridge (mammary line) or its differentiation into functional breast tissue.   - **Physiological Process:** Disruption in the morphogenesis of the mammary gland, which normally begins during the 6th week of gestation and continues through puberty.   - **Clinical Relevance:** Often associated with genetic syndromes (e.g., Poland syndrome, Rieger syndrome, or certain chromosomal abnormalities), and may be part of broader congenital malformation patterns.    **Conclusion:**   The phenotype is a congenital structural abnormality involving the complete or partial absence of breast tissue due to developmental failure, distinct from acquired or transient conditions. It is a specific, high-level morphological abnormality rooted in embryonic development, with implications for both clinical diagnosis and genetic evaluation.
