448;1;assistant;2230;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Aplasia/Hypoplasia of the thymus" describes a structural abnormality characterized by the complete absence (aplasia) or underdevelopment (hypoplasia) of the thymus gland.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Complete absence of thymic tissue (aplasia).     - Partial or reduced development of the thymus (hypoplasia), regardless of the degree of underdevelopment.     - This includes cases where the thymus is present but functionally inadequate due to structural underdevelopment.     - Applies to both congenital and acquired forms, though the primary context is congenital (as thymic hypoplasia/aplasia is typically developmental in origin).    - **Excluded:**     - Atrophy or involution of the thymus in aging (normal physiological process).     - Transient thymic shrinkage due to stress, infection, or steroid use (non-congenital, reversible).     - Isolated thymic dysplasia without measurable aplasia or hypoplasia (unless it results in functional deficiency).     - Thymic hyperplasia or neoplasia (opposite structural abnormalities).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Thymus gland, a primary lymphoid organ located in the anterior mediastinum.   - **Developmental Feature:** Congenital malformation affecting thymic organogenesis, often linked to disruptions in embryonic development (e.g., in DiGeorge syndrome, 22q11.2 deletion syndrome).   - **Physiological Process:** Impaired T-cell maturation and immune system development, leading to T-cell deficiency and increased susceptibility to infections.   - **Clinical Relevance:** This phenotype is a key feature in primary immunodeficiencies, particularly those involving T-cell lymphopenia and impaired adaptive immunity. It is often associated with other congenital anomalies (e.g., cardiac defects, facial dysmorphism) when part of a syndrome.  **Conclusion:**   The concept refers specifically to a congenital structural defect of the thymus involving either complete absence or inadequate development, with significant implications for T-cell development and immune competence. It is a precise, high-level phenotype in the context of developmental and immunological disorders.
