466;1;assistant;2310;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Abnormality of premolar" refers to any deviation from normal structure, development, number, or function of the premolar teeth. This includes congenital, developmental, or acquired abnormalities affecting one or more premolars.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Missing premolars (hypodontia or anodontia involving premolars)     - Extra premolars (supernumerary premolars)     - Malformed or dysplastic premolars (e.g., abnormal shape, size, or enamel defects)     - Erupted or unerupted premolars with abnormal positioning or alignment     - Premolars with delayed eruption or failure to erupt     - Premolars affected by developmental anomalies (e.g., fusion, gemination, dilaceration)     - Premolars with pathological changes (e.g., caries, resorption, trauma) if clinically relevant to the phenotype    - **Excluded:**     - Abnormalities limited to other tooth types (e.g., incisors, molars, canines) unless explicitly co-occurring     - General dental anomalies not specifically involving premolars (e.g., generalized enamel hypoplasia without premolar involvement)     - Non-dental conditions (e.g., systemic diseases with secondary dental effects, unless premolar-specific)    The concept is **moderately broad**—it captures any abnormality of the premolar teeth but does not specify the type, severity, or underlying mechanism. It serves as a parent term for more specific phenotypes (e.g., "Hypodontia of premolar", "Abnormality of premolar shape").  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Premolar teeth (second and first premolars, typically 4 per jaw, 8 in total in permanent dentition)   - **Developmental Feature:** Tooth development, eruption, morphogenesis, and odontogenesis   - **Physiological Process:** Dental mineralization, root formation, eruption dynamics   - **Clinical Characteristic:** Dental malocclusion, occlusal disturbances, aesthetic concerns, functional impairment (e.g., chewing), or diagnostic findings on radiographs    This concept is used in the context of genetic disorders, congenital syndromes, and dental developmental anomalies where premolar involvement is a key feature. It is part of the broader HPO hierarchy under "Abnormality of the dentition" and "Abnormality of the tooth."
