n the “Phenotypic Features” section of genetic testing reports, especially when describing immune system abnormalities in the context of a suspected monogenic disorder. - **Biomedical publications:** Used in phenotype descriptions of patients or cohorts, particularly in studies involving immunodeficiency or immune dysregulation syndromes. - **Patient education materials:** **Not used**. The phrase is too formal and lacks accessibility. Instead, patient-facing materials would use alternatives like “too many or too few T cells” or “abnormal immune cell levels.”  **4. Linguistic and Functional Assessment:**   - The phrase “Abnormality of T cell number” is **not** a natural expression in everyday conversation. It is a **descriptive clinical phrase**—a standard format used in medical and scientific writing to denote a deviation in a measurable biological parameter. - While the words are individually plain (e.g., “abnormality,” “number”), the **phrase as a whole** is syntactically and functionally characteristic of **clinical documentation and phenotype databases**, not of spontaneous speech or patient communication. - It is **not** a layperson synonym, despite its apparent simplicity. True layperson synonyms would be more conversational (e.g., “too many or too few T cells”).  **Conclusion:**   The synonym *“Abnormality of T cell number”* is **primarily used by healthcare professionals and researchers** in clinical, genetic, and scientific contexts—especially in structured documentation and phenotype databases. It is **not** a term used by patients or families. Its usage is driven by the need for standardized, concise, and unambiguous terminology in medical and research settings. While the wording appears plain, it functions as a **clinical descriptor** rather than a lay expression, and thus is **overwhelmingly used by professionals** in real-world medical communication.
