499;1;assistant;2203;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Fusion of gums" refers to the abnormal adhesion or union of the gingival tissues (gums) between adjacent teeth, resulting in a loss of the normal interdental papilla and potential narrowing or obliteration of the interdental space.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Direct tissue fusion (synechiae) between the gingival margins of neighboring teeth.     - Fusion that may occur at the level of the gingival sulcus or deeper connective tissue.     - Can be localized (e.g., between two specific teeth) or generalized.     - May result from developmental anomalies, trauma, or surgical intervention (e.g., post-extraction or periodontal surgery).    - **Excluded:**     - Fusion of teeth (dental ankylosis or gemination/syndactyly of tooth structures), which is a separate entity (e.g., HPO:0000677 "Tooth fusion").     - Gingival hyperplasia or inflammation without actual tissue fusion.     - Normal gingival architecture or transient gingival contact.    The concept is specific to soft tissue (gingival) fusion and does not encompass bony or dental fusion.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Gingiva (specifically the interdental gingiva and gingival sulcus).   - **Developmental Feature:** Can be congenital (e.g., due to abnormal development of the dental lamina or periodontal ligament) or acquired (e.g., post-traumatic or post-surgical).   - **Physiological Process:** Abnormal tissue adhesion or fibrosis in the periodontal soft tissues.   - **Clinical Relevance:** May lead to difficulties in oral hygiene, increased risk of periodontal disease, or aesthetic concerns. It is often observed in conditions such as gingival fibromatosis, certain syndromes (e.g., some forms of oral-facial-digital syndromes), or as a sequela of trauma or surgery.  **Conclusion:**   "Fusion of gums" denotes a pathological or developmental condition characterized by the abnormal union of gingival tissues between adjacent teeth, distinct from dental fusion or inflammation. It is a specific, clinically relevant phenotype involving the soft tissues of the periodontium, with implications for oral health and dental management.
