557;1;assistant;2205;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Fundic gland polyposis" refers to the presence of multiple polyps arising from the fundic glands of the gastric mucosa, specifically in the fundus and body of the stomach. These polyps are composed of hyperplastic fundic gland epithelium and are typically benign, non-neoplastic lesions.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Multiple polyps localized to the fundic glands (gastric glands in the fundus and body of the stomach).     - Histologically characterized by hyperplasia of fundic gland components (parietal cells, chief cells, mucous neck cells), without dysplasia or neoplastic transformation.     - Often associated with familial or syndromic conditions (e.g., familial fundic gland polyposis, PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome), but the concept itself describes the morphological phenotype regardless of etiology.    - **Excluded:**     - Polyps in other gastric regions (e.g., antral or pyloric glands) unless they involve fundic gland components.     - Neoplastic polyps (e.g., adenomas, carcinomas) or polyps with dysplasia.     - Non-gastric polyps (e.g., colorectal, duodenal).     - Single polyps or isolated fundic gland hyperplasia (unless multiple and clinically significant).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Gastric mucosa, specifically the fundic glands in the fundus and body of the stomach.   - **Pathological Process:** Hyperplasia of gastric fundic gland epithelium.   - **Developmental Feature:** Not a developmental anomaly per se, but may be associated with developmental syndromes involving epithelial proliferation.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** Often asymptomatic, detected incidentally during endoscopy, may be a marker for underlying genetic syndromes.    **Conclusion:**   "Fundic gland polyposis" is a specific morphological phenotype characterized by the presence of multiple, non-neoplastic polyps derived from the fundic glands of the proximal stomach.