559;1;assistant;2371;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Fibro-obliterative bile-duct lesion" describes a pathological condition characterized by the progressive replacement of normal bile duct structure with fibrous (scar) tissue, leading to obliteration (closure or blockage) of the bile duct lumen. This is a structural and pathological phenotype involving the bile ducts.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Fibrosis (excessive deposition of collagen and extracellular matrix) specifically within the walls of bile ducts.     - Obliteration (complete or near-complete loss of lumen patency) due to fibrotic remodeling.     - A chronic, progressive pathological process, typically associated with chronic injury, inflammation, or autoimmune mechanisms (e.g., in primary sclerosing cholangitis or post-infectious/traumatic cholangiopathy).     - The lesion is localized to the bile ducts (both intrahepatic and extrahepatic), not generalized fibrosis in the liver parenchyma.    - **Excluded:**     - Simple bile duct dilation or inflammation without fibrosis.     - Non-obliterative fibrosis (e.g., periductal fibrosis without lumen loss).     - Generalized liver fibrosis or cirrhosis not specifically involving bile duct obliteration.     - Bile duct neoplasia (e.g., cholangiocarcinoma), unless the lesion is primarily fibro-obliterative in nature.    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Bile ducts (intrahepatic and/or extrahepatic).   - **Pathological Process:** Fibrosis, tissue remodeling, and structural obliteration.   - **Clinical/Developmental Relevance:** This lesion is typically acquired, not congenital, and arises in the context of chronic cholangiopathies. It is a key histological feature in diseases such as primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC), biliary atresia (in some contexts), and post-surgical or post-infectious strictures.   - **Functional Consequence:** Impaired bile flow (cholestasis), potentially leading to jaundice, liver enzyme elevation, and progressive liver damage.    **Conclusion:**   The phenotype refers to a specific, histologically defined pathological process in bile ducts involving fibrotic obliteration of the ductal lumen. It is a precise, high-specificity concept within the HPO, capturing a distinct structural abnormality with clear pathological and clinical implications in biliary disease.
