611;1;assistant;2737;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Complete duplication of the proximal phalanx of the 5th toe" describes a congenital malformation characterized by the presence of two fully formed, structurally complete proximal phalanges in the fifth toe (the little toe), resulting in a duplicated skeletal element at the proximal level of the toe.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Specificity:** The concept is highly specific, targeting a precise anatomical anomaly at the level of the proximal phalanx of the fifth toe. It specifies "complete duplication," meaning both the bone and its associated articulations (with the metatarsal and the middle phalanx) are duplicated, not just partial or rudimentary remnants.   - **Inclusion:** Includes cases where there are two distinct, fully developed proximal phalanges in the fifth toe, typically leading to a bifid or duplicated toe structure. This may be associated with a double toe (syndactyly or polydactyly variant) involving the fifth toe.   - **Exclusion:** Excludes partial duplications (e.g., only a bony spur or osseous fragment), duplication of other phalanges (e.g., middle or distal), duplication of other toes (e.g., second or third toe), or duplication of the entire toe (which would involve multiple phalanges). Also excludes cases where duplication is due to fusion or other non-duplicative malformations.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** The proximal phalanx of the fifth toe — the first bone in the toe, articulating with the fifth metatarsal proximally and the middle phalanx distally.   - **Developmental Feature:** A congenital limb malformation arising during embryonic development, likely due to abnormal segmentation or duplication of the digital ray.   - **Clinical/Phenotypic Context:** This is a skeletal anomaly within the spectrum of polydactyly or digital duplication, specifically affecting the fifth toe. It may occur in isolation or as part of a broader syndrome (e.g., in