ion of the stomach" denotes a structural anomaly involving the abnormal spatial placement of the stomach, distinct from functional or morphological defects. It is a descriptive, anatomical phenotype relevant to developmental biology, gastrointestinal radiology, and clinical gastroenterology, particularly in the context of congenital malrotation or post-surgical complications.
