642;1;assistant;2105;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Otitis externa" refers to inflammation of the external ear canal, specifically the skin and subcutaneous tissues of the external auditory canal, extending from the ear opening (external auditory meatus) to the tympanic membrane (eardrum). It is a localized inflammatory condition affecting the outer ear.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:** Inflammation involving the external auditory canal, including symptoms such as pain, erythema, swelling, discharge, pruritus, and hearing loss due to canal obstruction. The condition may be acute or chronic and can be infectious (bacterial, fungal) or non-infectious (e.g., due to dermatitis, trauma, or allergic reactions).   - **Excluded:** Inflammation of the middle ear (otitis media), inner ear (labyrinthitis), or the tympanic membrane itself as a primary feature (though secondary involvement may occur). The concept does not include generalized ear infections or systemic inflammatory conditions affecting the ear.   - The concept is specific to the external ear and does not encompass broader otological or systemic conditions.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** External auditory canal (external ear), including the skin lining and associated structures (e.g., ceruminous glands, hair follicles).   - **Physiological Process:** Inflammatory response in the skin and subcutaneous tissues of the external ear canal.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** A clinical diagnosis characterized by localized pain, itching, discharge, and/or hearing impairment due to canal occlusion.   - **Developmental Feature:** Not inherently developmental, however, it can occur at any age and may be more common in certain populations (e.g., swimmers, individuals with eczema).    **Conclusion:**   "Otitis externa" in the HPO is a well-defined, anatomically specific phenotype referring to inflammation of the external ear canal, distinct from middle or inner ear pathology. It captures a clinically relevant condition with clear boundaries in terms of anatomical location and pathological process.
