895;1;assistant;2425;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "sensory perception of pain" refers to the physiological ability to detect and consciously experience painful stimuli. It encompasses the neural processes involved in the detection of noxious stimuli, their transmission to the central nervous system, and the resulting subjective experience of pain.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:** The concept includes the fundamental sensory component of pain—i.e., the perception of pain as a conscious experience arising from nociceptive input. This includes both acute and chronic pain perception, provided it involves the sensory-discriminative aspect (e.g., location, intensity, quality of pain).   - **Excluded:** It does not include emotional or affective responses to pain (e.g., suffering, fear, or distress), which are distinct components of the pain experience and are captured by other HPO concepts such as "abnormal emotional response to pain" or "pain-related anxiety." It also excludes pain threshold or tolerance as behavioral measures unless directly tied to sensory perception.   - The concept is moderately specific: it refers to the sensory modality of pain perception rather than a particular anatomical site or mechanism (e.g., neuropathic vs. inflammatory pain), but it is more specific than general "abnormal sensation" and more focused than "pain" alone.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Primarily involves peripheral nociceptors, sensory nerves (e.g., Aδ and C fibers), spinal cord dorsal horn, thalamus, and somatosensory cortex.   - **Physiological Process:** Nociception—the transduction, transmission, and perception of noxious stimuli.   - **Developmental Feature:** Not inherently developmental, however, it may be relevant in developmental disorders affecting sensory pathways.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** A core component of pain syndromes, neuropathies, and genetic disorders affecting sensory processing (e.g., hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies).    **Conclusion:**   The concept "sensory perception of pain" denotes the objective, sensory-discriminative aspect of pain—i.e., the ability to perceive pain as a distinct sensory experience—within the framework of nociceptive signaling and central processing. It is distinct from affective, cognitive, or behavioral responses to pain and is grounded in neurophysiological mechanisms of sensory perception.
