907;1;assistant;2454;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Abnormal choroid morphology" refers to a structural deviation in the choroid, the vascular layer of the eye located between the retina and the sclera. This includes any qualitative or quantitative alteration in the choroid’s shape, thickness, texture, or organization that deviates from normal.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:** Any non-standard structural appearance of the choroid, such as thinning, thickening, irregularities in layering, vascular abnormalities (e.g., tortuosity, dilation), or focal or diffuse changes detectable via imaging (e.g., OCT, angiography). This encompasses both congenital and acquired abnormalities.   - **Excluded:** Functional impairments of the choroid (e.g., reduced perfusion without structural change) unless they are explicitly linked to morphological alteration. Also excluded are abnormalities in adjacent structures (e.g., retina or sclera) unless they are directly attributable to choroidal morphology.   - The concept is **moderately specific**, as it captures a broad range of structural anomalies in the choroid but does not specify the underlying cause (e.g., genetic, inflammatory, degenerative) or the exact nature of the abnormality (e.g., choroidal neovascularization, atrophy).  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical structure:** Choroid (a highly vascularized, pigmented layer of the uveal tract).   - **Physiological process:** Vascular supply to the outer retina and RPE, regulation of ocular fluid dynamics and metabolic exchange.   - **Developmental feature:** Relevant in both developmental disorders (e.g., congenital choroidal hypoplasia) and age-related or disease-associated changes (e.g., in age-related macular degeneration, posterior uveitis, or inherited retinal dystrophies).   - **Clinical relevance:** Often detected through ophthalmic imaging (e.g., optical coherence tomography, fundus autofluorescence, angiography), and associated with visual impairment, photoreceptor dysfunction, or progression of retinal disease.  **Conclusion:**   "Abnormal choroid morphology" denotes a structural anomaly of the choroid, encompassing a wide range of imaging-defined deviations in its architecture. It is a phenotypic descriptor used in the context of ocular pathology, particularly in retinal and uveal disorders, and serves as a key indicator in diagnosing and classifying diseases affecting the posterior segment of the eye.
