910;1;assistant;2446;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Telangiectasia" refers to the presence of dilated, small blood vessels (capillaries, venules, or arterioles) that are visible on the skin or mucous membranes. These vessels appear as fine, red or purple lines, spots, or web-like patterns and are typically non-blanching (do not fade under pressure).  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Dilatation of superficial blood vessels (especially capillaries and post-capillary venules) in the skin or mucosa.     - Visible vascular lesions that are persistent and not transient (e.g., not due to temporary vasodilation from heat or trauma).     - Can occur in various anatomical locations (e.g., face, limbs, mucous membranes, gastrointestinal tract).     - May be generalized or localized.     - Includes both congenital and acquired forms, as long as the structural abnormality of vessel dilation is present.    - **Excluded:**     - Transient vascular changes (e.g., flushing, erythema due to inflammation or temperature).     - Larger vascular malformations (e.g., hemangiomas, arteriovenous malformations), which are classified separately in HPO.     - Spider angiomas (though they may resemble telangiectasia, they are distinct in morphology and pathogenesis and are classified under a different HPO term: "Spider angioma").     - Vascular changes due to trauma, infection, or drug reactions unless they represent persistent structural dilation.    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Superficial blood vessels (capillaries, venules) in the skin and mucous membranes.   - **Pathophysiological Process:** Abnormal vessel wall integrity or regulation leading to persistent dilation.   - **Developmental Feature:** Can be congenital (e.g., in hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia) or acquired (e.g., in chronic sun exposure, liver disease, or connective tissue disorders).   - **Clinical Relevance:** A key feature in several genetic syndromes (e.g.,