923;1;assistant;2214;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Moderate sensorineural hearing impairment" describes a specific degree of hearing loss characterized by damage or dysfunction of the inner ear (cochlea) or the auditory nerve (cranial nerve VIII), resulting in reduced ability to perceive sound. The term "moderate" specifies the severity level within the sensorineural hearing loss spectrum.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Hearing impairment due to pathology in the cochlea or auditory nerve (sensorineural etiology).     - Hearing loss with a pure-tone average (PTA) typically in the range of 41–60 dB HL (decibels hearing level), which defines "moderate" severity according to audiometric standards.     - Bilateral or unilateral involvement, though the concept does not specify laterality unless otherwise indicated.     - Functional impact on speech perception and communication, particularly in noisy environments.    - **Excluded:**     - Conductive hearing loss (due to outer/middle ear pathology).     - Mixed hearing loss (combined sensorineural and conductive components).     - Mild (26–40 dB HL) or severe (61–80 dB HL) sensorineural hearing loss.     - Non-hearing-related auditory processing disorders (unless explicitly linked).    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Inner ear (cochlea), auditory nerve (vestibulocochlear nerve), or central auditory pathways (if central auditory neuropathy is involved).   - **Physiological Process:** Auditory transduction, neural signal transmission, or central auditory processing.   - **Developmental Feature:** Can be congenital or acquired at any age, may be progressive or stable.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** Measured via audiometry, often associated with difficulty understanding speech, especially in background noise, but not complete deafness.    **Conclusion:**   The concept refers specifically to a moderate degree of sensorineural hearing loss, defined by audiometric thresholds and etiology, excluding conductive and other non-sensorineural forms. It captures a clinically relevant, quantifiable phenotype within the broader category of hearing impairment, with clear boundaries in severity and mechanism.
