aring loss, particularly when associated with external auditory canal atresia.   - **Relevance:** This concept is used in clinical genetics, dysmorphology, and pediatric otolaryngology to describe and classify ear malformations in the context of genetic syndromes or isolated anomalies.  **Conclusion:**   "Microtia, second degree" denotes a specific, moderately severe congenital malformation of the external ear with defined structural hypoplasia, excluding milder or more severe forms, and is contextually rooted in embryonic development and structural anatomy of the outer ear.
