931;1;assistant;2525;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept labeled "Chronic sinusitis" refers to a persistent inflammatory condition of the paranasal sinuses characterized by ongoing symptoms lasting for more than 12 weeks despite medical treatment. It is a chronic form of sinusitis, distinct from acute or recurrent episodes.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Inflammation of the mucosal lining of the paranasal sinuses (e.g., maxillary, ethmoid, frontal, sphenoid) that persists for ≥12 weeks.     - Clinical features such as nasal congestion, facial pain/pressure, purulent nasal discharge, and reduced sense of smell (hyposmia/anosmia) that are continuous or frequently recurring.     - Histopathological evidence of chronic inflammation (e.g., lymphocytic infiltration, goblet cell hyperplasia, subepithelial fibrosis) may be present, though not required for clinical diagnosis.     - Excludes acute sinusitis (duration <12 weeks) and recurrent acute sinusitis (≥4 episodes per year with symptom-free intervals).    - **Excluded:**     - Acute or subacute sinusitis.     - Sinusitis due to non-inflammatory causes (e.g., neoplasms, structural obstruction without inflammation).     - Sinus symptoms secondary to systemic conditions (e.g., immunodeficiency, cystic fibrosis) unless the inflammation is the primary feature.    **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Paranasal sinuses (primarily maxillary, ethmoid, frontal, and sphenoid sinuses).   - **Physiological Process:** Chronic inflammation of the sinus mucosa, involving dysregulated immune responses, impaired mucociliary clearance, and persistent microbial colonization (e.g., biofilms).   - **Developmental Feature:** Not inherently developmental, however, it may be associated with congenital or acquired anatomical abnormalities (e.g., deviated septum, nasal polyps) that predispose to chronicity.   - **Clinical Characteristic:** A chronic clinical syndrome defined by duration, symptom b