939;1;assistant;2583;**Phenotype Identification:**   The HPO concept "Gastrointestinal eosinophilia" refers to the abnormal accumulation of eosinophils—a type of white blood cell—in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract. This is a histopathological finding, typically observed in tissue biopsies, and indicates an eosinophilic inflammatory response within the GI mucosa, submucosa, or muscularis layers.  **Scope and Specificity:**   - **Included:**     - Eosinophil infiltration in any segment of the GI tract (esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine).     - The presence of eosinophils at levels exceeding normal physiological thresholds (typically >15–20 eosinophils per high-power field in mucosal biopsies, though thresholds may vary by location).     - This includes both localized and diffuse involvement, regardless of the underlying cause (e.g., allergic, parasitic, autoimmune, or idiopathic).   - **Excluded:**     - Eosinophilia limited to the bloodstream (hypereosinophilia) without GI tissue involvement.     - Eosinophilic infiltration due to systemic conditions not primarily involving the GI tract (e.g., hypereosinophilic syndrome with secondary GI manifestations, unless the GI involvement is the primary feature).     - Eosinophilic infiltration in other organ systems without GI involvement.     - Transient or reactive eosinophilia not associated with histological evidence in GI tissue.  **Biomedical Context:**   - **Anatomical Structure:** Gastrointestinal tract (mucosa, submucosa, muscularis propria).   - **Pathological Process:** Eosinophilic inflammation, a type of immune-mediated tissue response.   - **Clinical Relevance:** This finding is associated with conditions such as eosinophilic esophagitis, eosinophilic gastritis, eosinophilic gastroenteritis, and may also occur in food allergies, parasitic infections, or autoimmune disorders. It is a key diagnostic criterion in several eosinophilic gastrointestinal disorders (EGIDs).   - **Developmental Feature:** Not inherently developmental, can occur at any age, though some EGIDs show age-specific prevalence (e.g., eosinophilic esophagitis in children and young adults).    **Conclusion:**   "Gastrointestinal eosinophilia" is a precise, histologically defined phenotype indicating eosinophil-rich inflammation within the gastrointestinal tract. It is not a disease per se but a pathological hallmark used to classify and diagnose eosinophilic gastrointestinal disorders. The concept is specific to tissue-level eosinophil accumulation in the GI system and excludes systemic or non-GI eosinophilic conditions.
